From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 04:04:09 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: um: map a vDSO whose entries are plain system calls

An arm64 guest task had no vDSO: no [vdso] mapping and no
AT_SYSINFO_EHDR. A C library that reaches clock_gettime only through
the vDSO, as the glibc 2.39 inside a guest application's own root file
system does, then failed every clock with -1 and errno 0, and time()
returned stack garbage.

Build the vDSO as arm64 Linux's and the x86 UML port's are built, an ELF
shared object linked at address 0 with one read-only segment, and embed
it in the kernel. It carries the LINUX_2.6.39 version node, both hash
tables (glibc and bionic read the GNU one, musl the SysV one), the Linux
note, and an unwind table, and it needs no relocation. Its entries are
__kernel_clock_gettime, __kernel_gettimeofday and __kernel_clock_getres.
Each loads the call number, executes svc and returns the call's result
unchanged, a negative errno included, as the native vDSO's fallback
does. None reads a counter: the guest has none a task may read, and a
fast path is a topic of its own. __kernel_rt_sigreturn is not exported;
a handler without SA_RESTORER keeps returning through the [sigpage].

exec maps the image as a special mapping where the address space has
room, in addition to the [sigpage], and records a move by mremap().
ARCH_DLINFO puts its address in AT_SYSINFO_EHDR; it is now defined in
every configuration and holds AT_MINSIGSTKSZ only with SME. An AArch32
task keeps its kuser helpers and compat sigpage and gets neither a vDSO
nor the entry, as on native arm64.

UML_ARM64_VDSO selects it. The x86-64 userspace slice clears it, since
its tasks run x86 code. The comments of the time-data headers that said
the guest has no vDSO now describe this one.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
